Z||000008||fastback87||11-22-2001||09:25 PM||||what are the different jets in the T-fitting for then.  The jets that go in the T-fitting are labeled 50hp, 70hp, and 90?hp.  And there isn't anything in the big blue fitting on the NOS jet?????  And what about the platinum plugs?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shaun41178||11-22-2001||10:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fastback87:<BR><B>what are the different jets in the T-fitting for then.  The jets that go in the T-fitting are labeled 50hp, 70hp, and 90?hp.  And there isn't anything in the big blue fitting on the NOS jet?????  And what about the platinum plugs?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The different jets in the T fitting regulate the fuel pressure when the nitrous is activated.  The smaller the jet in the T fitting the more pressure it will add to the rail adn more fuel be injected.  Larger jet and it allows less fuel in through the existing injectors.  It has a .042 in it now and it has given good results.  It is what NOS recommends on a stock 2.8 engine.  So don't play with that.<P>NO NO NO!.  The jets are not labeled 50-70 or 90 hp.  It doesn't say that on there anywhere?  How did you come up with that?  THey have numbers such as .032, .036 and .040.  Look at them closely and you will see these numbers.  The jets are not labeled in hp cause on larger engines the same jets won't add the same amount of power.<P>If there is not a jet in the blue fan spray nozzle then you need to put one in there.  I would recommend the .032 to start.<P>Call NOS or email them and tell them you need install instructions for kit number 5120.  If you don't get an instrction manual then you will probably never be able to get it hooked up right since you haven't seen how it was done originally.  There is only so much I can do through a computer.<P>If you can't get it figured out and are planning on coming down for the Daytona show then bring the kit with you and a few small tools and I will hook it up for you.  Should only take me about 2-3 hours.<P>Or call me at 904-615-9916 and I guess I could try to talk you through it.<P>In the meantime do some research on the web for nitrous applications.  I would recommdend the links at the bottom of my webpage that Fropuff recommended earlier above.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fastback87||11-23-2001||12:40 PM||||On the back of the little packages someone wrote, 50hp, 75hp, and 90?hp.  I recall seeing the other numbers you are talkin about .032 or something on the front of the package that was factory printed.  There are three different packages and all had jets in them.  One is missing and it is in the T-Fitting....I don't know why but it is there.  Now are you saying that it shouldn't be there and it should be in the blue NOS jet that bolts to the breather tube??  I am not mechanically stupid at all I also have all the connections pretty well figured out except for the Vacuum line which you guys have helped me with.  I know I can install this thing pretty easily but I want to make sure the settings are very mild, for now.  At least until I get another daily driver.  So If you could just help me out, by telling me why the jets are in the wrong places now and how they should be then I will be fine, I am sure :-) or at least as fine as I can be with a fiero nitro rocket.  I will look up on Holley for the   5120  Kit and see if they have any info.  Thanks again everyone.||||reg||12
Z||000011||Smoooooth GT||11-23-2001||01:04 PM||||Fastback...<BR> It's in the T to regulate the Fuel.... Then put the 50hp fitting in the Fogger Nozzle!! LOL.. I think!! I'm still loooking for the book.... Sorry man!!     Steve<P>ps. Between you, me, and Shaun... we'll figue it out!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smooooth_Yellow_sig_3.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/texas_1.gif">          <BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
